course: dictionary
lesson: 1358
title: Candyfloss: one sweet, many names
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have different names in different countries, so a word you know may be called something else elsewhere. Look for clues in the sentence around the word and in the words that describe it.
explain: The example sentence says, «candyfloss is cotton candy (US)». The word «cotton candy» tells you that the American name is different, but both words mean the same fluffy sweet.
explain: Describing words are clues too. Fluffy, pink or white, wound onto a stick, sold at fairs: each of these tells you what candyfloss looks like and where you find it.
fact: Candyfloss is a fluffy pink or white sweet made of spun sugar.
fact: Candyfloss is wound onto a stick and sold at fairs, circuses and the seaside.
fact: In the USA it is called cotton candy, and in Australia it is also called fairy floss.
fact: Scientists can show how it is made: sugar is melted and spun into thin threads that cool and set.
fact: Teachers mention candyfloss when they talk about sugar and teeth.
exercise: Where do children often eat candyfloss? | In a hospital ward | On a train to work | At school lunch | *At the fair or the seaside
exercise: «Candyfloss is cotton candy (US).» What is cotton candy called in the USA? | Gobstopper | Toffee apple | Jelly bean | *Candyfloss
exercise: Fluffy, pink and wound onto a stick. What sweet is this? | *Candyfloss | Lollipop | Toffee | Chewing gum
test: What is candyfloss mostly made of? | Melted chocolate | *Spun sugar | Crushed biscuits | Fresh cream
test: What is candyfloss called in Australia? | Toffee | Sugar cane | Cotton candy | *Fairy floss
exam: Which place is candyfloss NOT usually sold? | At the seaside | At a circus | At a fair | *In a bank
